grades.requirement:
Research how Indian government departments and public agencies recommend verifying schemes, benefits, grants, and job opportunities. Compare official Government of India and State/UT portals, ministry websites, recruitment authorities, government publications, official notifications, and verified announcement channels against common misinformation sources, fake websites, fraudulent recruitment portals, and misleading social-media claims.
Create an India-specific verification checklist covering:

whether the website belongs to the relevant government department or authorized agency;
whether the opportunity appears on an official government portal;
whether eligibility and application requirements match the official notification;
whether the source requests inappropriate fees, personal information, OTPs, or payments;
whether the notification has a current publication/update date;
whether the opportunity can be cross-checked with the responsible ministry or department;
how suspicious websites, messages, or recruitment claims can be reported through Indian government channels.
Verification sources:
National Portal of India: https://www.india.gov.in/
PIB Fact Check: https://factcheck.pib.gov.in/
Press Information Bureau: https://www.pib.gov.in/
National Career Service: https://www.ncs.gov.in/
National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal: https://www.cybercrime.gov.in/
Do not use U.S. FTC, UK government, or other foreign scam examples as evidence for Indian government opportunities.

Analyze Indian government opportunities according to whether they provide immediate assistance or may create longer-term financial, educational, employment, health, housing, or business benefits. Compare direct financial support, skill-development programs, government employment, business-support programs, education assistance, healthcare benefits, housing support, and financial-inclusion initiatives.
Use specific Indian programs as representative examples and distinguish between:
- immediate monetary or in-kind assistance;
- reduced household expenses or risk;
- improved employability or income potential;
- longer-term asset or business-building potential.
Do not make universal claims about wealth creation. Base conclusions on the documented design, eligibility, benefits, obligations, and available outcome evidence for the Indian programs examined.

Apply the evaluation and scoring framework developed in Step 10 to the strongest current or recurring Indian opportunities identified during the research.
Compare government jobs, skill-development programs, education assistance, healthcare benefits, entrepreneurship support, housing programs, financial-assistance initiatives, and other relevant Indian opportunities.
Create an impact-versus-eligibility matrix showing which specific Indian opportunities appear to offer the greatest potential long-term benefit relative to effort, qualification requirements, application complexity, risk, obligations, and likelihood of eligibility.
Separate opportunities into practical categories such as:
- high-priority actions;
- opportunities worth monitoring;
- conditional opportunities requiring additional eligibility information;
- low-priority options.
For every shortlisted current opportunity, verify its current status using the responsible Indian government ministry, department, official portal, or relevant State/UT government source. Record the official source and the date on which its current status was verified.
Do not recommend applying for an opportunity when eligibility cannot be established from the available information; instead, identify the missing eligibility condition that must be checked.
